<header><p class="enc-kicker">Historical factor</p><h1 class="enc-title">State religion and conversion</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">State religion and conversion refers to the recurring pattern in which rulers adopted or imposed a religion to unify subjects and legitimise power. Conversions of rulers could transform the culture of whole regions. Examples include the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aksumite-empire">Aksumite Empire</a>'s adoption of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavid Empire</a>'s imposition of Shia <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a>'s conversion to Islam and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazar Khaganate</a>'s adoption of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a>.</p></div>
